// Romance: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== romance.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Moving Pavement
genre: romance + period-drama
tone: Gaslit, hopeful and gently comic
era: 1900 | period
setting: The Exposition Universelle, Paris | real | city
logline: A mechanic who keeps the Exposition's moving pavement running falls for a kora player from the Senegal pavilion, in a summer when the whole world has come to Paris and neither of them is allowed to leave their post.
premise: A wooden pavement two miles long circles the fair at five miles an hour, and somebody has to oil it. {lucien} rides it all day with a can and a spanner. {awa} plays for visitors in a reconstructed village she finds absurd. They can meet for the eleven minutes it takes the pavement to pass her pavilion.
synopsis: {lucien} is twenty-six and has never left the fourteenth arrondissement; the fair is the furthest he has travelled, and it comes to him on a belt. {awa} is the daughter of a griot family from Saint-Louis, hired with her brother for six months to be looked at. The first act is the eleven minutes: he rides past, she plays, he gets off, he is fined for leaving the platform. They have no language in common until she turns out to speak better French than he does. The second act is a courtship conducted on a conveyor. He shows her the Palace of Electricity from the roof at night; she shows him that the village is a stage set and teaches him to hear the difference between a song for tourists and a song for home. Her brother {moussa} distrusts him on principle and is right about how the city sees them. {lucien}'s foreman, {gaspard}, who has a daughter of marrying age, offers him a permanent place with the Metro when the fair ends. The fair will end: in November it will all be pulled down, and her contract takes her home. He asks her to stay. She asks him what, precisely, she would be in Paris once she is no longer an exhibit. He has no answer, and to his credit knows it. In the third act the pavement breaks down on the last night with ten thousand people aboard, and he fixes it with her holding the lamp. He does not take the Metro job. The last shot is a quay at Bordeaux and a third-class ticket; whether it is hers or his the film shows only by which of them is carrying the kora.
question: When two people meet in a place built to be temporary, which of them should have to become a foreigner?
themes: love under exhibition; work as a way of seeing; who travels and who is displayed
audience: Adults; anyone who likes a romance with its eyes open
viewpoint: Shared; the pavement gives the film its tracking shots
stakes: Two livelihoods, a family's trust, and what each would have to give up to keep the other
conflict: Two people who suit each other against a city that has decided in advance what each of them is
ending: He fixes the pavement, refuses the safe job and goes with her. She carries the tickets; he carries the kora.
experience: Giddy motion, a slow recognition of what is unfair, and a leap that feels earned
look: Electric light on water, plaster palaces, wooden walkways; long gliding takes at five miles an hour
sound: A kora over machinery, fairground organs, twenty languages in a crowd
m: belong | community | belonging-vs-identity | transformed
@ lucien | Lucien Bardet | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 26 | To be more than the man with the oil can | The world has come to his city and he is still underneath it | He has nothing to offer her but Paris | Handy, shy, incurious until now | From wanting her to stay to going himself | Parisian slang, tongue-tied near her | Blue overalls, oil can, a cap he keeps taking off
@ awa | Awa Kouyate | co-lead | love-interest | human | she/her | 23 | To finish the contract and go home with the money | Her family's music is being sold as scenery | She likes him, which was not in the contract | Sharp, proud, amused by the French | From guarded to choosing him on her terms | Precise French, drier than his | Indigo boubou for the pavilion, her own shawl after hours
@ moussa | Moussa Kouyate | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 29 | To bring his sister home unharmed | He has seen how the visitors look at her | He cannot forbid her anything | Protective, dignified, funny when he relents | From hostility to handing over a second kora string | Formal French, Wolof asides | Grand boubou, arms folded
@ gaspard | Gaspard Mollet | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 52 | A reliable man for the Metro, and a son-in-law | He likes {lucien} | He is offering a good life that is the wrong one | Bluff, kindly, conventional | Unchanged; he shakes hands and is puzzled | Shop-floor bark | Bowler, a foreman's watch
~ lucien - awa | mechanic and musician | eleven minutes a circuit | one ticket between them, and a kora
~ awa - moussa | sister and brother | he watches every move | he gives her the spare string
~ lucien - gaspard | workman and foreman | a job for life offered | a handshake and a refusal
